
  So the honest version of this feature in a single-user app is [1mtime-based[0m: a due date arrives, something goes overdue,
  a recurring task comes back around. That's a real, useful feature. It's just not the same feature the original
  sentence described, and I'd rather say so now than hand you a notification bus with nothing to publish to it.

  There's also an ordering problem. [1mThere are no tasks.[0m No model, no storage, no UI to show them. Notifications are a
  layer over a task system that doesn't exist. I'd decompose this as:

  - [1mProject 1 — the tasks app.[0m Task model, browser persistence, create/edit/complete/delete UI. Includes due dates,
    since notifications depend on them.
  - [1mProject 2 — reminders/notifications.[0m Built on Project 1's model and events.

  Each gets its own spec and plan. Project 2 is not designable until Project 1's shape is settled — its data model [3mis[0m
  the notification system's input.

  One more thing worth knowing before I propose approaches: browser notifications have a hard constraint. A closed tab
  can't fire anything. Real "your task is overdue" alerts that reach you when the app isn't open need a Service Worker
  plus the Push API plus a permission prompt — and push genuinely needs a server, which we just ruled out. Without one,
  notifications only fire while the tab is open.
